are 8, plus melds 6, total 14. So I have 8 hand tiles and 2 melds (6 tiles), total 14. So I need to discard one tile to get to 13, then wait for winning tile. So I must choose one tile to play from my hand.\n\nSo from my 8 hand tiles, I need to discard one. The valid actions are Play B6, Play T6, Play J1, Play B4, Play W3. So I can discard any of these.\n\nGiven my strategy to aim for All Pungs and Dragon Pung, I should keep the pairs for pungs. So I should discard a lone tile: either W3 or B6. Between W3 and B6, which is less useful? W3 might form a sequence with W1-W2-W3, but W1 is dead (0 left), W2 has 1 left, so very unlikely. B6 might form a sequence with B4-B5-B6, but B5 dead, so unlikely. Both are not useful for sequences. But B6 is in the same suit as B4 pair, so if I draw another B4, I could have a B4 pung, and then B6 becomes lone. Or if I draw B6, I could have a B6 pair? But I already have a B6 single, drawing another would make a pair, but then I would have three pairs (T6, B4, J1, and B6), which might be okay, but I need to eventually reduce to one pair. However, keeping B6 might allow a B6 pung if I draw two more? Unlikely. Discarding B6 seems fine.\n\nAlso, considering defense: what tiles are dangerous? The across player has melds including T3-T4-T5, W1-W2-W3, B7 pung. So they might be close to winning. They have taken chows, so they might be going for a hand with chows. Discarding tiles that they might need for sequences could be risky. They have already taken W1-W2-W3, so W3 is already used in their meld, so discarding W3 is probably safe since they already have it. B6: they have a B7 pung, so they might need B6 for a sequence with B5-B6-B7? But B5 is dead (0 left), so unlikely. B6 might be safe.\n\nI think discarding W3 or B6 is similar. I'll choose B6 because it's a simple tile and less likely to be needed by others.\n\nReason: I aim to complete a hand with All Pungs and Dragon Pung for sufficient points. Keeping all pairs (T6, B4, J1) maximizes pung potential. Discarding the lone B6 maintains flexibility and is safe given dead surrounding tiles.\n\nAnswer: Play B6"}
